1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:01,818
Welcome to Resgrid Academy.

2
00:00:01,982 --> 00:00:05,607
In this lesson, we will record routine
checks and missed work.

3
00:00:05,813 --> 00:00:09,976
Paper checks make it difficult to see
which apparatus was inspected and

4
00:00:09,976 --> 00:00:11,814
which scheduled checks were missed.

5
00:00:11,961 --> 00:00:15,676
We will explain the features, the setup
decisions, and a

6
00:00:15,677 --> 00:00:17,791
practical way to check the result.

7
00:00:18,606 --> 00:00:23,305
A daily apparatus check is scheduled for
each engine, and the weekly compliance

8
00:00:23,307 --> 00:00:25,249
report shows any missed checks.

9
00:00:25,447 --> 00:00:28,669
Recorded checks show what was inspected
and what was missed.

10
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:32,800
The examples in this series use a
fictional department and are teaching

11
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:35,246
scenarios, not default settings.

12
00:00:36,077 --> 00:00:37,335
Checklist Templates.

13
00:00:37,549 --> 00:00:39,941
Ready-made checklists for common checks.

14
00:00:40,197 --> 00:00:43,755
Start from a template or build a checklist
for each apparatus.

15
00:00:44,571 --> 00:00:45,419
Checklists.

16
00:00:45,638 --> 00:00:47,388
Checklists members complete.

17
00:00:47,575 --> 00:00:50,899
Create the checklists members complete on
paper today.

18
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:53,669
Checklist schedules and due work.

19
00:00:53,833 --> 00:00:56,007
Recurring schedules and due checks.

20
00:00:56,251 --> 00:00:59,107
Schedule each checklist and choose who
completes it.

21
00:00:59,291 --> 00:01:02,111
Availability also depends on Checklists.

22
00:01:02,331 --> 00:01:04,563
Check access in the target department.

23
00:01:05,352 --> 00:01:06,345
New Checklist.

24
00:01:06,521 --> 00:01:07,812
Create a checklist.

25
00:01:08,006 --> 00:01:11,437
Pick the checks you do on paper today and
who completes them.

26
00:01:12,252 --> 00:01:14,213
Checklist compliance reports.

27
00:01:14,441 --> 00:01:17,648
Completed, skipped and overdue checks over
time.

28
00:01:17,871 --> 00:01:21,310
Pick the checks you do on paper today and
who completes them.

29
00:01:21,523 --> 00:01:24,348
Availability also depends on Checklists.

30
00:01:24,568 --> 00:01:26,792
Check access in the target department.

31
00:01:27,606 --> 00:01:30,497
For the training scenario, work in this
order.

32
00:01:30,719 --> 00:01:35,190
Step 1. Start from a checklist template or
build the approved checks.

33
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:39,433
Step 2. Assign the checklist and set its
recurring schedule.

34
00:01:39,643 --> 00:01:45,199
Step 3. Complete a demo check, record a
defect, and review compliance.

35
00:01:46,011 --> 00:01:47,661
Now verify the result.

36
00:01:47,887 --> 00:01:52,161
Compare due checks with completed,
skipped, and overdue results.

37
00:01:52,401 --> 00:01:57,473
Confirm defects have an assigned follow-up
owner. A common problem to watch for:

38
00:01:57,616 --> 00:02:00,832
A completion report documents recorded
checks.

39
00:02:01,021 --> 00:02:05,371
A failed item still requires action
through the department repair process.

40
00:02:06,165 --> 00:02:07,991
Your practice task is simple.

41
00:02:08,183 --> 00:02:12,435
Complete a daily Engine 1 check and leave
a second demo check overdue.

42
00:02:12,656 --> 00:02:15,801
Confirm that the compliance view
distinguishes both.

43
00:02:15,993 --> 00:02:20,513
Record the result, who owns the follow-up,
and when it should be reviewed.

44
00:02:20,650 --> 00:02:24,570
Use the linked feature scripts for the
detailed screen recording plan.

